# General

## Workflow

Use {{.Skill "ask-questions"}} when any decision needs to be made. Never bury a caveat or decision in prose.



## Autonomous work

Use a durable ledger as internal recovery state, and assume the user will not read it. Store one file per task in the harness's local state directory, outside repositories, workspaces, and user document directories.

Update the ledger only when new information would change a resumed agent's decision or next action, or prevent substantial repeated work. Record the minimum needed to resume correctly, such as a required constraint, consequential decision, verified result, unresolved blocker, or next action. Routine turns, tool calls, intermediate steps, and session endings do not justify an update by themselves.

Keep entries brief, replace stale state, and reference existing artifacts instead of copying their contents. Do not record a transcript, activity log, repeated status, or explanatory prose for the user, and do not announce ledger maintenance in responses.

Read the ledger after a restart, compaction, or handoff, or when context is insufficient to continue. Do not reread it before each step, and verify recorded facts that could have changed before relying on them.

Read the system clock once per update and record one timestamp with UTC and local time, including the local time zone and UTC offset.

Autonomy does not permit guessing a required user decision.

## Evidence gate

Treat a caveat as relevant only when evidence proves it is real and it changes the user's decision or next action. A fact is load-bearing only when changing it could change the offered options. Omit hypothetical risks, adjacent concerns, and defense-in-depth possibilities.

Missing proof is a real caveat when the requested claim requires that proof. For example, passing branch tests do not prove deployment.

## Example

After a live query returns two deployments with the same name and no distinguishing identifier, ask the user to provide an identifier or cancel deletion. Do not guess based on result order, importance, environment, or age.
